#
#   # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   # publish: the only job that touches a release. It runs after every build leg
#   # has succeeded, downloads their artifacts, then creates the release and
#   # attaches the lot.
#   #
#   # This inverts what Forgejo forced. There, `create-release` had to run first
#   # so the parallel build jobs could each attach their own asset, because
#   # Forgejo does not implement the upload-artifact@v4 protocol and there was no
#   # way to hand a built file between jobs. The cost was a window in which a
#   # release existed with some or none of its assets: a build that failed after
#   # `create-release` succeeded left a published, half-populated release behind.
#   #
#   # It also reverses a deliberate decision recorded on the `deb` matrix --
#   # "publish whichever architecture succeeded rather than losing both to one
#   # failure". That is now all-or-nothing. For a release that seems the better
#   # trade: a release carrying the amd64 .deb but not the arm64 one is a trap for
#   # arm64 users, who get "no such package" from apt rather than a clear "this
#   # version was not released". `fail-fast: false` stays on both matrices, so a
#   # broken leg still reports alongside the others rather than cancelling them.
#   #
#   # NOT PORTED: every API call below still speaks Forgejo. Note especially that
#   # `SERVER_URL: ${{ github.server_url }}` now resolves to https://github.com
#   # while the scripts append Forgejo's `/api/v1/...` paths, so these steps would
#   # 404 rather than publish anywhere at all. Fixing that means deciding where
#   # releases and the apt repository live -- both are still on
#   # git.armeafamily.com. Until then a `v*` tag pushed here builds everything and
#   # fails at this job, which is the safe direction to fail in.
